{"qid":"Q71747","names":["Duchy of Brittany"],"title":"Duchy of Brittany","endonym":{"native":"Duché de Bretagne","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Duchy of Brittany emerged as a distinct political entity after Alan II ('Twisted-Beard') reunited the Breton lands and expelled Viking settlers, with formal ducal title consolidating around 939. It maintained a precarious independence between the French and English crowns throughout the medieval period, governed by a succession of ducal houses including the Montfort line, which prevailed after the War of Breton Succession (1341–1365). At its height the duchy controlled the entire Breton peninsula, operated its own parlement, and enforced a distinct legal tradition. Anne of Brittany's marriage to Charles VIII of France in 1491 and subsequently to Louis XII triggered the personal union that led to formal incorporation into the French crown by the 1532 Edict of Union.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The duchy's territorial extent is well attested from the 10th century onward through French royal records and Breton charters; its precise boundaries fluctuated and are approximated in most cartographic reconstructions.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Brittany","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Brittany"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Duchy of Brittany","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duchy_of_Brittany"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Duchy of Brittany","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Alan II \"Barbetorte\"","nativeName":"Alain II Barbetorte","roman":"","start":938,"end":952,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"na